WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.470 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  what new york lacked in crowds  it made up for in hope in the city that  normally welcomes the world  this year the message was to stay away  as new york reflected on a year  they would love to forget the few  invited were frontline workers  like baldwin who lost his twin brother  to covid19  the pair had worked together patrolling  times square for 19 years  his brother died before their 50th  birthday this summer  i was in my house with my family and i  felt something i said my  you know he my calvin just died they  said don't say  that and no more five minutes later the  doctor called i believe that if he would  have survived the first wave and caught  it the second way  he probably would have survived it  because the first way nobody didn't know  how to treat it  more than 25 000 new yorkers who brought  in  2020 lost their lives in the pandemic  even without the safety concerns there  felt little reason to celebrate  the turning of this year was marked with  grief  and grace the crowd stretched for nearly  20 blocks here this time last year but  like almost everything  coronavirus has cast a shadow over times  square  2020 will be studied by scientists and  historians in the years to come  but for the millions of new yorkers who  live the pandemic  it will be defined by moments of  personal loss  and resilience for calvin who works as  an emergency room doctor  and lost his grandfather to covet 19.  carrying out tests for guests at times  square  feels like a break i've seen things that  will give you nightmares for the rest of  your life  what makes our experience unique here in  new york city  is that we went through the worst of the  worst in march and april  but we also rebounded really quickly and  people of the world can look to us right  now especially in new year's eve  not just to see a ball drop but also new  yorkers coming together and realizing  with this small space we can work  together to  you know make a world you know more  hopeful and that's that's all i could  want for 2021.  the stroke of midnight will not cure  coronavirus  america is still battling a fierce  second wave  but donald trump struck a victorious  tone over vaccines  which are being rolled out woefully  slowly  in the face of great challenges this  year americans showed incredible grit  strength tenacity and resolve and  together we achieve truly historic  victories like  nobody ever thought possible  the crisis will long outlive the current  administration  but with 2020 in the rearview mirror  there is a sense here of immense hope  and relief  sally lockwood sky news new york

